s Treatise on the Response of the Tao was written by the twelfth-century sage Li Ying-chang. Presenting foundational teachings and practices of the Action and Karma school of Taoism, it is replete with folk stories illustrating the teachings and an introductory essay that discusses the more esoteric meanings of the passages.\n\nTold with clarity and depth, these seminal Taoist teachings offer guidance on leading a balanced, healthy life."},"latest_revision":9,"revision":9,"created":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2008-04-01T03:28:50.625462"},"last_modified":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2026-05-17T21:08:47.323361"}}},"ISBN:9780877735441":{"bib_key":"ISBN:9780877735441","info_url":"http://openlibrary.org/books/OL2217857M/Seven_Taoist_masters","preview":"borrow","preview_url":"https://archive.org/details/seventaoistmaste0000unse","thumbnail_url":"https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/671292-S.jpg","details":{"publishers":["Shambhala","Distributed in the United States by Random House"],"number_of_pages":178,"subtitle":"a folk novel of China","isbn_10":["0877735441"],"covers":[671292],"local_id":["urn:sfpl:31223102571545","urn:sfpl:31223102571552","urn:sfpl:31223032354111","urn:sfpl:31223102571628","urn:sfpl:31223102571560","urn:sfpl:31223102571586","urn:sfpl:31223102571602","urn:bwbsku:O8-DCF-623"],"lc_classifications":["BL1923 .S44 1990"],"key":"/books/OL2217857M","publish_places":["Boston","[New York]"],"contributions":["Wong, Eva, 1951-"],"languages":[{"key":"/languages/eng"},{"key":"/languages/chi"}],"pagination":"xxiii, 178 p. :","source_records":["marc:marc_records_scriblio_net/part20.dat:159703082:702","marc:marc_openlibraries_sanfranciscopubliclibrary/sfpl_chq_2018_12_24_run02.mrc:30672294:2701","marc:marc_loc_2016/BooksAll.2016.part19.utf8:117667185:793","amazon:0877735441","ia:seventaoistmaste0000unse","promise:bwb_daily_pallets_2022-08-30","marc:marc_columbia/Columbia-extract-20221130-011.mrc:19962988:980","marc:marc_columbia/Columbia-extract-20221130-002.mrc:439542273:1069","marc:harvard_bibliographic_metadata/ab.bib.01.20150123.full.mrc:738402736:746","marc:harvard_bibliographic_metadata/20220215_038.bib.mrc:305019694:1350"],"title":"Seven Taoist masters","dewey_decimal_class":["299/.5144"],"identifiers":{"librarything":["556533"],"goodreads":["501290"]},"edition_name":"1st ed.","lccn":["89043307"],"subjects":["Spiritual life -- Taoism."],"publish_date":"1990","publish_country":"mau","by_statement":"translated by Eva Wong.","works":[{"key":"/works/OL19196226W"}],"type":{"key":"/type/edition"},"ocaid":"seventaoistmaste0000unse","oclc_numbers":["20692417"],"latest_revision":14,"revision":14,"created":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2008-04-01T03:28:50.625462"},"last_modified":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2026-05-10T09:44:17.504442"}}},"ISBN:9781611802412":{"bib_key":"ISBN:9781611802412","info_url":"http://openlibrary.org/books/OL27209341M/Being_Taoist","preview":"noview","preview_url":"http://openlibrary.org/books/OL27209341M/Being_Taoist","details":{"other_titles":["Huainan zi. Selections. English."],"table_of_contents":[{"level":0,"label":"","title":"Introduction","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"The Taoist in public life. The art of leadership: the view of vastness","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"The Taoist in domestic life. The art of healthy living: embracing simplicity","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"The Taoist in private life. The art of concealment: hidden sky","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"The Taoist in spirit life. The art of being free: free and easy wandering.","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}}],"description":{"type":"/type/text","value":"\"A beautifully clear and accessible explanation of how to live a Taoist life--by reknowned Taoist master Eva Wong. Being Taoist is one of the most readable books on Taoist philosophy available. It shines a light on exactly what it takes to live a Taoist life. Taoist living rests on four pillars--the public, the domestic, the private, and the spirit lives. Not only do Taoists strive to live these four aspects fully and in a balanced way, they also believe there is an outlook and an art to each of them. Eva Wong uses the teachings of Taoist masters (one for each pillar) to explain the essential concepts. She then gives voice to these texts--simplifying them, removing barriers to understanding, and making them completely accessible and relevant to the modern reader. Wong is a clear and enthusiastic guide to this intriguing spiritual Way, and she challenges us to stop, reflect, and ask ourselves: Do we balance the public, domestic, private, and spirit aspects in our lives? Do we emphasize some at the expense of the others? Do we ever think about unifying worldly and spiritual wisdom in our lives?\"--"},"links":[{"url":"9781611802412.jpg","title":"Cover image"}],"isbn_10":["1611802415"],"lc_classifications":["BL1923 .B36 2015","BL1923.B36 2015"],"key":"/books/OL27209341M","authors":[{"key":"/authors/OL448094A","name":"Eva Wong"}],"subtitle":"wisdom for living a balanced life","subjects":["RELIGION","Taoist)","Taoism (see also PHILOSOPHY","Spiritual life","PHILOSOPHY","Taoist","Taoism"],"isbn_13":["9781611802412"],"pagination":"vi, 193 pages","source_records":["marc:marc_openlibraries_sanfranciscopubliclibrary/sfpl_chq_2018_12_24_run05.mrc:314243391:3840","bwb:9781611802412","marc:marc_loc_2016/BooksAll.2016.part41.utf8:167319019:2174","promise:bwb_daily_pallets_2024-05-14:P9-DWH-229"],"title":"Being Taoist","dewey_decimal_class":["299.5/1444"],"number_of_pages":193,"languages":[{"key":"/languages/eng"}],"lccn":["2014019273"],"local_id":["urn:sfpl:31223116640849","urn:sfpl:31223116640831","urn:sfpl:31223116640856","urn:bwbsku:P9-DWH-229"],"publish_date":"2015","publish_country":"mau","by_statement":"edited and translated by Eva Wong","o